1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,840
We're going to discuss the power of hypothesis tests.

2
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:23,400
The power of hypothesis tests. So, a little introduction.

3
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:37,400
So far, you'll have met some number of hypothesis tests and it may not have occurred to you to

4
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:51,400
ask the question, what makes a good hypothesis test?

5
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:57,400
What makes one hypothesis test preferable to another one?

6
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:03,400
In particular, if we had, as we may have seen so far, more than one hypothesis test that

7
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:09,400
we could use in a given situation, well, what makes one test better than another?

8
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:17,400
Moreover, could we define the notion of a test being optimal for a given situation in the

9
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:21,400
sense that it's better than any other test?

10
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:25,400
How should we choose a test? How can we create a test?

11
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:33,400
Well, these are rather deep ideas, a little beyond what we can discuss satisfactorily here.

12
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:42,400
But, let's start by just recapping our basic theory of hypothesis testing.

13
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:47,400
So, we have some null hypothesis, which is the default.

14
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:54,400
It's there to be shot down, but it's what we'll accept unless data come along that make us feel

15
00:01:54,400 --> 00:02:06,400
that, via our test, some alternative hypothesis that I denote by HA is more plausible than the null.

16
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:17,400
So, the null hypothesis is akin to the defendant in a criminal trial in North American and English law.

17
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:22,400
So, really, what does a null hypothesis look like?

18
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:29,400
Well, usually we have some parameter, let's just assume we're interested in a single parameter.

19
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:36,400
And, typically in statistics we denote our parameters by Greek letters, let's say theta.

20
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:42,400
And, the null hypothesis typically defines a single value for that parameter.

21
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:52,400
So, it assigns some default value to that unknown parameter, in which case we call the null hypothesis simple.

22
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:57,400
It's just assigning some single value to the parameter that we don't know.

23
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:06,400
So, this could be the mean of some distribution, or the probability of an event occurring, or a standard deviation, or whatever our test may be about.

24
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:21,400
The alternative hypothesis is usually not simple, in that it does not define a single alternative value.

25
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:41,400
It may simply be the negation of the null hypothesis, so it says theta isn't theta naught, or it may be that we have only greater than, or equal to, or less than, as cases to consider.

26
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:48,400
In which case we say that the alternative is composite.

27
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:54,400
Composite in that it's collecting together more than one value of the primitive interest.

28
00:03:54,400 --> 00:04:15,400
So, we have a null hypothesis, there to be shot at, data come along, we summarize the data via a test statistic, we perform some sort of test, and on that we decide whether we should reject the null hypothesis, or not reject.

29
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:25,400
And again, akin to a court of law, we do have two possible errors that can occur.

30
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:40,400
Now, a type 1 error is defined to be the event where we reject H0, when it was true.

31
00:04:40,400 --> 00:05:05,400
The probability of a type 1 error is denoted by alpha, and it's called the significance level of the test.

32
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:17,400
So, this is rather like rejecting, so it's rejecting the null hypothesis when it's true, it's rather like condemning an innocent defendant in a criminal trial.

33
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:30,400
A type 2 error is that we don't reject, we fail to reject.

34
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:36,400
Now notice that I'm falling short of saying we accept the null hypothesis.

35
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:53,400
Remember, the null hypothesis typically defines a specific value on some unknown parameter, and maybe I'll never accept that some unknown parameter, so the mean of some population, is exactly equal to some value that I've specified.

36
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:55,400
Maybe I'll never accept that.

37
00:05:55,400 --> 00:06:04,400
But it may be that it appears acceptable in that data do not come along that oblige me to reject that hypothesis.

38
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:19,400
So a type 2 error occurs when we fail to reject the null hypothesis, when in fact the null hypothesis was false.

39
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:32,400
And the probability of a type 2 error is, well guess what, we denoted by beta.

40
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:39,400
So there are these two errors, two types of errors, both of which we'd like to avoid.

41
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:49,400
Now, if we think about this a little further, we recognise that a type 1 error is an event conditional on the null hypothesis being true.

42
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:52,400
But our null hypothesis typically tells us what's going on.

43
00:06:52,400 --> 00:07:03,400
It tells us the value of the null hypothesis, at least in the case, it tells us the value of the unknown parameter, rather, in the case where it's simple, which is usually the case.

44
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:08,400
So we know what's going on when H0 is true.

45
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:19,400
The type 2 error, on the other hand, says, hmm, now we're conditioning on H0 being false.

46
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:23,400
And what's the probability of rejecting when H0 is false?

47
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:35,400
Well, looking at the form of our alternative hypothesis, that won't specify exactly what's going on, because usually the alternative is composite.

48
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:38,400
It doesn't tell us exactly what's going on.

49
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:42,400
It sets a range of values for the unknown parameter.

50
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:51,400
So, these two types of errors are rather different in their characteristics.

51
00:07:55,400 --> 00:08:01,400
It's helpful to define something called the power function.

52
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:16,400
The power of a test is just one minus the probability of making a type 2 error.

53
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:18,400
So, as the name would suggest, power's good.

54
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:19,400
We like power.

55
00:08:19,400 --> 00:08:20,400
We want high power.

56
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:23,400
And we don't want to make a type 2 error.

57
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:27,400
So, it's one minus the probability of making a type 2 error.

58
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:45,400
And we can think of this as the ability of the test to detect the null hypothesis when it's false.

59
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:54,400
So, the ability of the test to weedle out a false null hypothesis.

60
00:08:54,400 --> 00:09:00,400
So, based on this idea, we define something that we call the power function.

61
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:04,400
And I'll denote it by pi of theta.

62
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:09,400
So, it's defined to be, I'll use that notation, because we've not seen this before.

63
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:15,400
And this is the probability of rejecting the null hypothesis.

64
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:20,400
Now, for a given test, this will depend on all sorts of things, not least the sample size.

65
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:28,400
But let's be quite clear, the probability of rejecting the null hypothesis, as I've suggested on the left-hand side here,

66
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:35,400
it depends on the value of the unknown parameter.

67
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:42,400
So, this is a function that depends on what the value of the parameter may be.

68
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:48,400
But the beauty is, we can consider it for every possible value of the parameter, should we wish.

69
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:55,400
And it's the probability of rejecting the null hypothesis for that particular value of theta.

70
00:09:55,400 --> 00:10:02,400
So, we would like this power function to be large, so it would have a high probability,

71
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:05,400
on values of theta that are consistent with the alternative,

72
00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:14,400
and are low, so a low probability of rejection, on values of theta that are consistent with H0.

73
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:17,400
What's the ideal?

74
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:27,400
Well, the ideal is a function that says, well, it has no probability of rejecting

75
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:36,400
if theta, abusing notation slightly, is in the set of values that's consistent with the null hypothesis.

76
00:10:36,400 --> 00:10:41,400
So, if theta happens to be in that set, well, no chance of rejecting.

77
00:10:41,400 --> 00:10:51,400
One, on the other hand, so we're certain to reject if theta is in the set of values that's consistent with the alternative hypothesis.

78
00:10:51,400 --> 00:11:00,400
That would be the ideal. So, if a test could attain that, it would be optimal, in an absolute sense.

79
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:12,400
But, in practice, unless we're in really trivial situations, in practical situations, no tests are ideal.

80
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:21,400
No tests are going to satisfy that their power function looks like this guy.

81
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:26,400
Well, let's have a look at an example in some detail.

82
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:34,400
So, we have some random variable x, and we'll just say we've got three observations from this random variable.

83
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:46,400
So, three independent observations from x, and our case we'll look at is that x is a binomial variable,

84
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:49,400
or it's a special case, it's a Bernoulli variable.

85
00:11:49,400 --> 00:11:55,400
We're tossing a coin once, if you like, and observing heads with probability p.

86
00:11:55,400 --> 00:12:11,400
More generally, I might say that x takes the value 1, with probability p, and 0, with probability 1 minus p.

87
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:18,400
So, maybe we observe a success if we see a 1, and a failure if we don't.

88
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:27,400
We'll start off by looking at the following hypotheses.

89
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:34,400
P is a probability, it's an unknown parameter, we know it has to be between 0 and 1.

90
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:39,400
Let's suppose our null hypothesis specifies that it's between 0 and a half inclusive,

91
00:12:39,400 --> 00:12:51,400
and our alternative is that it's between 0 and 1.

92
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:56,400
These are not the most natural hypotheses to consider in this sort of situation.

93
00:12:56,400 --> 00:13:00,400
We'll come to the most natural case presently, but let's have a look at this for the starters.

94
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:02,400
And we're going to compare two tests.

95
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:20,400
So, test 1, let's say it rejects the null hypothesis when we see three successes in our three observations.

96
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:27,400
Well, each observation is a success with probability p, whatever p is, we observe three successes.

97
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:35,400
So, each one with probability p would suggest that, let's say this has power function, let's call it pi 1,

98
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:41,400
and the power function is going to be p times p times p, assuming these three observations are independent of one another,

99
00:13:41,400 --> 00:13:44,400
and so the power is p cubed.

100
00:13:44,400 --> 00:14:08,400
Test 2, on the other hand, will reject the null hypothesis when we see two or three successes.

101
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:12,400
So, let's say it has power function pi 2.

102
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:20,400
Well, we might see three successes, which we know happens with probability p cubed,

103
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:24,400
or, and mutually exclusive, so we can just use our additive rule,

104
00:14:24,400 --> 00:14:32,400
or with probability 3 p squared 1 minus p, we observe two successes.

105
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:44,400
So, what does our power functions look like in this case?

106
00:14:44,400 --> 00:14:49,400
So, let's plot the power function, just the probability rejecting the null hypothesis.

107
00:14:49,400 --> 00:14:56,400
We know that p has to go between 0 and 1, and both of the tests are such that, of course,

108
00:14:56,400 --> 00:15:02,400
if p is 0, it's 0, and if it's 1, it's 1.

109
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:11,400
Pi 1 is just p cubed, and so that looks something like this over the range.

110
00:15:11,400 --> 00:15:16,400
Can't do it all that carefully, but...

111
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:24,400
And pi 2 looks a little bit more like this over the range.

112
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:30,400
So, of course, given the nature of the function, if we look at it, it can't be smaller.

113
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:34,400
It's an increasing function of p.

114
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:43,400
So, pi 2 is always at least pi 1.

115
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:51,400
So, pi 2 suggests that, it tells us that test 2 always has a higher power function.

116
00:15:51,400 --> 00:16:06,400
But that's not so good in situations where, well, the null hypothesis is true,

117
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:08,400
which is when we're over here.

118
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:11,400
We don't really want to be rejecting the null hypothesis in that situation.

119
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:26,400
So, this test will very often lead to a false rejection of the null hypothesis when it's actually true.

120
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:30,400
Let's just, continuing to look at this example,

121
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:37,400
let's look at more natural alternative, let's look at more natural hypotheses than we've chosen here,

122
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:40,400
and compare the same two tests.

123
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:52,400
So, we're not going to, we're going to keep these two tests, but let's consider the more natural case.

124
00:16:52,400 --> 00:17:03,400
The null hypothesis states that p is a half, so it's like we're tossing an unbiased coin,

125
00:17:03,400 --> 00:17:10,400
and the alternative is that, let's just say it's not a half.

126
00:17:10,400 --> 00:17:22,400
Now, in that case, we have simple, so we have a simple null hypothesis,

127
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:29,400
and we know that test 1, which has lower power for all values of p,

128
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:34,400
we know it's going to have a significance level.

129
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:41,400
Well, that's the probability that we reject the null hypothesis,

130
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:47,400
and we reject the null hypothesis in test 1 when we see three successes,

131
00:17:47,400 --> 00:17:51,400
and we see three successes with probability, well, under the null hypothesis,

132
00:17:51,400 --> 00:18:00,400
a half times a half times a half, so a half cubed, so it has significance level,

133
00:18:00,400 --> 00:18:06,400
let's call it alpha 1, an eighth.

134
00:18:06,400 --> 00:18:13,400
But test 2 has significance level, well, we get two or three heads,

135
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:23,400
so we get three heads with probability an eighth, or two heads,

136
00:18:23,400 --> 00:18:28,400
so that's actually got significance level a half.

137
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:33,400
So, if we go back to our picture, and the picture's not very good,

138
00:18:33,400 --> 00:18:39,400
but when p's a half, test 2 actually has, which is not very good to scale,

139
00:18:39,400 --> 00:18:50,400
you can sketch them better yourself, but has a power of a half,

140
00:18:50,400 --> 00:18:54,400
and we reject with probability a half.

141
00:18:54,400 --> 00:19:02,400
And so, although test 2 has higher power for all values of p,

142
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:10,400
for this particular case, that significance level looks rather too high.

143
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:15,400
We wouldn't probably be very happy, to beg the question,

144
00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:19,400
but we wouldn't be very happy doing a test which, when the null hypothesis were true,

145
00:19:19,400 --> 00:19:26,400
would reject the null hypothesis half the time.

146
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:36,400
In this way, we can compare two tests by doing a power analysis,

147
00:19:36,400 --> 00:19:40,400
and I hope that this little lecture has given you that flavour.

148
00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:44,400
So, this is an introduction to the idea of the power of a hypothesis test,

149
00:19:44,400 --> 00:19:50,400
and I hope that you've taken something from it, and enjoyed it.

150
00:19:50,400 --> 00:19:52,400
Thank you.

